{"type": "message", "id": "a9d120d1", "parentId": "421e7da9", "timestamp": "2026-04-16T14:17:38.181Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:10", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"esophageal cancer 5 year survival rate\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 976,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9e25c6158f01dfef\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival Rates for Esophageal Cancer | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"9e25c6158f01dfef\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/esophagus-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5b65e2ee980857bb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of esophageal cancer is 60%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 60%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5b65e2ee980857bb\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"89c6d54ce4286401\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCancer Stat Facts: Esophageal Cancer - SEER\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"89c6d54ce4286401\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/esoph.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b38f6b65b236ff6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe earlier esophageal cancer is ... diagnosed. For esophageal cancer, <strong>18.6% are diagnosed at the local stage. The 5-year relative survival for localized esophageal cancer is 48.7%</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b38f6b65b236ff6\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"seer.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2b3eed3489db9a85\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEsophageal Cancer: An Updated Surveillance Epidemiology and End Results Database Analysis - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"2b3eed3489db9a85\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7141161/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"17663bf3050eaca1\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEsophageal cancer is the sixth leading cause of cancer-related deaths and the eighth most common cancer worldwide with a 5-year survival rate of <strong>less than 25%</strong>. Here we report the incidence, risk factors and treatment options that are available currently, and moving into the future.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"17663bf3050eaca1\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c76a6e370c3dcddd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival statistics for esophageal cancer | Canadian Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c76a6e370c3dcddd\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://cancer.ca/en/cancer-information/cancer-types/esophageal/prognosis-and-survival/survival-statistics\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ae9f1345848991bf\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIt is used to give an estimate of the percentage of people who will survive cancer. In Canada, the 5-year net survival for esophageal cancer is 18%. This means that, on average, <strong>about 18% of people diagnosed with esophageal cancer will survive for at least 5 years</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ae9f1345848991bf\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"cancer.ca\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1aa1d33a1da5a7ca\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFive-Year Survival Rates - SEER Training Modules\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1aa1d33a1da5a7ca\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://training.seer.cancer.gov/ugi/intro/survival.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"781bd0968a12f2bf\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEsophageal cancer is lethal because the esophagus has no serosa; any tumor extension beyond the esophagus can spread rapidly. Overall, the five-year survival rate is <strong>less than 10%</strong> for all stages combined.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"781bd0968a12f2bf\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"training.seer.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6035b1d2c3948e1d\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEsophagus Cancer Survival Rate: Five-Year, Relative, and More\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6035b1d2c3948e1d\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.healthline.com/health/esophagus-cancer-survival-rate\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0f09c0529a8e5d4a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, a relative survival ... (SEER) database, the five-year survival rate for people with esophageal cancer is <strong>19.3 percent</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"0f09c0529a8e5d4a\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"March 22, 2019\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.healthline.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e8e81bc82abbdfc7\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStages of Esophageal Cancer | Texas Oncology\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e8e81bc82abbdfc7\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.texasoncology.com/cancer-care/esophageal-cancer/stages\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1058c1f509b7d8c8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn one large clinical study of 160 patients, the average survival duration was 11 months following treatment with surgery alone. In another study involving 110 patients with squamous cell cancer and 124 with adenocarcinoma, the average survival was 16 months, the 5-year survival rate was <strong>20%</strong> and ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1058c1f509b7d8c8\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.texasoncology.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b1ec2bb8c681960b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nEsophageal Cancer Treatment (PDQ®) - NCI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b1ec2bb8c681960b\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.gov/types/esophageal/hp/esophageal-treatment-pdq\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"afb57ca6b9e3ff54\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurgical treatment of resectable esophageal cancers results in 5-year survival rates of <strong>5% to 30%</strong>, with higher survival rates in patients with early-stage cancers.[8] Asymptomatic small tumors confined to the esophageal mucosa or submucosa are detected only by chance.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"afb57ca6b9e3ff54\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"363983f2c7e59309\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival outcomes and prognostic factors of early-onset and late-onset metastatic esophageal cancer: a population-based study | Scientific Reports\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"363983f2c7e59309\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-08618-7\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ddd499c0b49e7b28\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStudies have shown that the response rate to standard chemotherapy in patients with advanced EC is <strong>between 20 and 35%, with a median survival of only 6–10 months</strong>.17 Approximately 50% of EC patients have distant lymph node or organ metastasis ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ddd499c0b49e7b28\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"July 2, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.nature.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1a28ee4913ae7c85\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival for oesophageal cancer | Cancer Research UK\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1a28ee4913ae7c85\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/oesophageal-cancer/survival\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3d8c7fb23d121067\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe NHS, other health organisations, and researchers collect information. They record what happens to people with cancer in the years after their diagnosis. 5 years is a common time point to measure survival.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3d8c7fb23d121067\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancerresearchuk.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "esophageal cancer 5 year survival rate", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 976, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9e25c6158f01dfef\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival Rates for Esophageal Cancer | American Cancer Society\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9e25c6158f01dfef\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/esophagus-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5b65e2ee980857bb\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of esophageal cancer is 60%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 60%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5b65e2ee980857bb\">>>", "siteName": "www.cancer.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"89c6d54ce4286401\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nCancer Stat Facts: Esophageal Cancer - SEER\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"89c6d54ce4286401\">>>", "url": "https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/esoph.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b38f6b65b236ff6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe earlier esophageal cancer is ... diagnosed. For esophageal cancer, <strong>18.6% are diagnosed at the local stage. The 5-year relative survival for localized esophageal cancer is 48.7%</strong>....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b38f6b65b236ff6\">>>", "siteName": "seer.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2b3eed3489db9a85\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEsophageal Cancer: An Updated Surveillance Epidemiology and End Results Database Analysis - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"2b3eed3489db9a85\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7141161/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"17663bf3050eaca1\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEsophageal cancer is the sixth leading cause of cancer-related deaths and the eighth most common cancer worldwide with a 5-year survival rate of <strong>less than 25%</strong>. Here we report the incidence, risk factors and treatment options that are available currently, and moving into the future.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"17663bf3050eaca1\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c76a6e370c3dcddd\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival statistics for esophageal cancer | Canadian Cancer Society\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c76a6e370c3dcddd\">>>", "url": "https://cancer.ca/en/cancer-information/cancer-types/esophageal/prognosis-and-survival/survival-statistics", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ae9f1345848991bf\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIt is used to give an estimate of the percentage of people who will survive cancer. In Canada, the 5-year net survival for esophageal cancer is 18%. This means that, on average, <strong>about 18% of people diagnosed with esophageal cancer will survive for at least 5 years</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ae9f1345848991bf\">>>", "siteName": "cancer.ca"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1aa1d33a1da5a7ca\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFive-Year Survival Rates - SEER Training Modules\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1aa1d33a1da5a7ca\">>>", "url": "https://training.seer.cancer.gov/ugi/intro/survival.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"781bd0968a12f2bf\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEsophageal cancer is lethal because the esophagus has no serosa; any tumor extension beyond the esophagus can spread rapidly. Overall, the five-year survival rate is <strong>less than 10%</strong> for all stages combined.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"781bd0968a12f2bf\">>>", "siteName": "training.seer.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6035b1d2c3948e1d\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEsophagus Cancer Survival Rate: Five-Year, Relative, and More\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6035b1d2c3948e1d\">>>", "url": "https://www.healthline.com/health/esophagus-cancer-survival-rate", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0f09c0529a8e5d4a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, a relative survival ... (SEER) database, the five-year survival rate for people with esophageal cancer is <strong>19.3 percent</strong>....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0f09c0529a8e5d4a\">>>", "published": "March 22, 2019", "siteName": "www.healthline.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e8e81bc82abbdfc7\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStages of Esophageal Cancer | Texas Oncology\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e8e81bc82abbdfc7\">>>", "url": "https://www.texasoncology.com/cancer-care/esophageal-cancer/stages", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1058c1f509b7d8c8\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIn one large clinical study of 160 patients, the average survival duration was 11 months following treatment with surgery alone. In another study involving 110 patients with squamous cell cancer and 124 with adenocarcinoma, the average survival was 16 months, the 5-year survival rate was <strong>20%</strong> and ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1058c1f509b7d8c8\">>>", "siteName": "www.texasoncology.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b1ec2bb8c681960b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nEsophageal Cancer Treatment (PDQ®) - NCI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b1ec2bb8c681960b\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/types/esophageal/hp/esophageal-treatment-pdq", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"afb57ca6b9e3ff54\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurgical treatment of resectable esophageal cancers results in 5-year survival rates of <strong>5% to 30%</strong>, with higher survival rates in patients with early-stage cancers.[8] Asymptomatic small tumors confined to the esophageal mucosa or submucosa are detected only by chance.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"afb57ca6b9e3ff54\">>>", "siteName": "www.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"363983f2c7e59309\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival outcomes and prognostic factors of early-onset and late-onset metastatic esophageal cancer: a population-based study | Scientific Reports\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"363983f2c7e59309\">>>", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-08618-7", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ddd499c0b49e7b28\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStudies have shown that the response rate to standard chemotherapy in patients with advanced EC is <strong>between 20 and 35%, with a median survival of only 6–10 months</strong>.17 Approximately 50% of EC patients have distant lymph node or organ metastasis ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ddd499c0b49e7b28\">>>", "published": "July 2, 2025", "siteName": "www.nature.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1a28ee4913ae7c85\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival for oesophageal cancer | Cancer Research UK\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1a28ee4913ae7c85\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancerresearchuk.org/about-cancer/oesophageal-cancer/survival", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3d8c7fb23d121067\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe NHS, other health organisations, and researchers collect information. They record what happens to people with cancer in the years after their diagnosis. 5 years is a common time point to measure survival.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3d8c7fb23d121067\">>>", "siteName": "www.cancerresearchuk.org"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1776349058180}}
{"type": "message", "id": "20d40aba", "parentId": "a9d120d1", "timestamp": "2026-04-16T14:17:38.182Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:11", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"liver cancer 5 year survival rate hepatocellular carcinoma\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 967,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a38a12d70a4b8d76\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLiver Cancer Survival Rates | American Cancer Society | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a38a12d70a4b8d76\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/liver-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fc98901999740cd4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of liver cancer is 30%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 30%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fc98901999740cd4\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"efd15535351f513f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival Rates » Hepatitis B Foundation\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"efd15535351f513f\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.hepb.org/research-and-programs/liver/staging-of-liver-cancer/survival-rates/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b84cb7f19a3557d9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe 5-year survival rate from liver cancer varies from about <strong>10% to 14%</strong>. This survival rate is low partly because many people with liver cancer also have cirrhosis, which itself is a serious liver disease.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b84cb7f19a3557d9\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.hepb.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5e3a7edc28dcf133\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe Actual Five-year Survival Rate of Hepatocellular Carcinoma Patients after Curative Resection - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5e3a7edc28dcf133\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2687566/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6da2886097cfe927\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe five-year survival rate of patients after curative resection of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) has been reported to be <strong>30 to 50%</strong>, however the actual survival rate may be different. We analyzed the actual 5-year survival rate and prognostic factors after curative resection of HCC.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6da2886097cfe927\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"959d3f5076a0eda3\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) - Survival rates - Mayo Clinic\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"959d3f5076a0eda3\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/hepatocellular-carcinoma/survival-rates/gnc-20589115\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c26a3b565ced7ce8\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLearn about the symptoms, diagnosis and treatment for this type of liver cancer.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c26a3b565ced7ce8\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 30, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.mayoclinic.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c34687f10cdbae9e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCancer of the Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct - Cancer Stat Facts\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"c34687f10cdbae9e\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/livibd.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3da62507246caeeb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe earlier liver and intrahepatic ... The 5-year relative survival for localized liver and intrahepatic bile duct cancer is <strong>37.6%</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3da62507246caeeb\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"seer.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb9df2171123c3b6\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nPrognostic Scores and Survival Rates by Etiology of Hepatocellular Carcinoma: A Review - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cb9df2171123c3b6\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10181349/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1238c2a6db2dda67\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a common cancer and ranks sixth among all malignancies worldwide. Risk factors for HCC can be classified as infectious or behavioral. Viral hepatitis and alcohol abuse are currently the most common risk factors for HCC; however, nonalcoholic liver disease is expected to become the most common cause of HCC in upcoming years.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"1238c2a6db2dda67\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e363ef9e61148552\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLife expectancy of patients with hepatocellular carcinoma according to the upfront treatment: A nationwide analysis - ScienceDirect\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e363ef9e61148552\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2211568423000025\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ddbb69192d7a87ec\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\n... The best survival rates in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma are achieved with liver transplantation, whether performed as a first or as subsequent step, with a 5-year survival rate of <strong>approximately 80%</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"ddbb69192d7a87ec\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"January 20, 2023\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.sciencedirect.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3f13bcf7a1b3e5ab\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nHepatocellular Carcinoma Life Expectancy - Rare Disease Advisor\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"3f13bcf7a1b3e5ab\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.rarediseaseadvisor.com/hcp-resource/hepatocellular-carcinoma-life-expectancy/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a1d9fa9b38612acf\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn recent years, the 5-year overall survival rate has improved from 18% to 22%,1 and <strong>may exceed 50%</strong> with currently available treatment options, according to a nationwide analysis conducted in France.2 · HCC accounts for approximately 90% of ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"a1d9fa9b38612acf\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"September 19, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.rarediseaseadvisor.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8f20a3ea85b67e35\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLiver Cancer Survival Rates | Roswell Park Comprehensive Cancer Center - Buffalo, NY\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8f20a3ea85b67e35\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.roswellpark.org/cancer/liver/survival-rates\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f7439eb7dceec64b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDistant disease. Among patients with disease that has spread to distant body areas at the time of diagnosis, 5-year survival is <strong>3.5%</strong>. Learn more liver cancer survival statistics from the National Cancer Institute.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f7439eb7dceec64b\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.roswellpark.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"71d6c69e9d270ac9\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLiver Cancer: Symptoms, Signs, Causes & Treatment\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"71d6c69e9d270ac9\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9418-liver-cancer\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d48386d0ca68596a\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFurther, researchers break down survival rates by cancer stage. The five-year survival rate for early-stage (stage I or stage II) liver cancer is <strong>37%</strong>. That survival rate drops to 13% for stage III liver cancer and 3% for stage IV liver cancer.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d48386d0ca68596a\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"August 30, 2023\",\n      \"siteName\": \"my.clevelandclinic.org\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "liver cancer 5 year survival rate hepatocellular carcinoma", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 967, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a38a12d70a4b8d76\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLiver Cancer Survival Rates | American Cancer Society | American Cancer Society\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a38a12d70a4b8d76\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/liver-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fc98901999740cd4\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of liver cancer is 30%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 30%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fc98901999740cd4\">>>", "siteName": "www.cancer.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"efd15535351f513f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival Rates » Hepatitis B Foundation\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"efd15535351f513f\">>>", "url": "https://www.hepb.org/research-and-programs/liver/staging-of-liver-cancer/survival-rates/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b84cb7f19a3557d9\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe 5-year survival rate from liver cancer varies from about <strong>10% to 14%</strong>. This survival rate is low partly because many people with liver cancer also have cirrhosis, which itself is a serious liver disease.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b84cb7f19a3557d9\">>>", "siteName": "www.hepb.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5e3a7edc28dcf133\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe Actual Five-year Survival Rate of Hepatocellular Carcinoma Patients after Curative Resection - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5e3a7edc28dcf133\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2687566/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6da2886097cfe927\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe five-year survival rate of patients after curative resection of hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) has been reported to be <strong>30 to 50%</strong>, however the actual survival rate may be different. We analyzed the actual 5-year survival rate and prognostic factors after curative resection of HCC.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6da2886097cfe927\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"959d3f5076a0eda3\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) - Survival rates - Mayo Clinic\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"959d3f5076a0eda3\">>>", "url": "https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/hepatocellular-carcinoma/survival-rates/gnc-20589115", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c26a3b565ced7ce8\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLearn about the symptoms, diagnosis and treatment for this type of liver cancer.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c26a3b565ced7ce8\">>>", "published": "December 30, 2025", "siteName": "www.mayoclinic.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c34687f10cdbae9e\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nCancer of the Liver and Intrahepatic Bile Duct - Cancer Stat Facts\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"c34687f10cdbae9e\">>>", "url": "https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/livibd.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3da62507246caeeb\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe earlier liver and intrahepatic ... The 5-year relative survival for localized liver and intrahepatic bile duct cancer is <strong>37.6%</strong>....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3da62507246caeeb\">>>", "siteName": "seer.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb9df2171123c3b6\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nPrognostic Scores and Survival Rates by Etiology of Hepatocellular Carcinoma: A Review - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cb9df2171123c3b6\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10181349/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1238c2a6db2dda67\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is a common cancer and ranks sixth among all malignancies worldwide. Risk factors for HCC can be classified as infectious or behavioral. Viral hepatitis and alcohol abuse are currently the most common risk factors for HCC; however, nonalcoholic liver disease is expected to become the most common cause of HCC in upcoming years.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"1238c2a6db2dda67\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e363ef9e61148552\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLife expectancy of patients with hepatocellular carcinoma according to the upfront treatment: A nationwide analysis - ScienceDirect\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e363ef9e61148552\">>>", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2211568423000025", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ddbb69192d7a87ec\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\n... The best survival rates in patients with hepatocellular carcinoma are achieved with liver transplantation, whether performed as a first or as subsequent step, with a 5-year survival rate of <strong>approximately 80%</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"ddbb69192d7a87ec\">>>", "published": "January 20, 2023", "siteName": "www.sciencedirect.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3f13bcf7a1b3e5ab\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHepatocellular Carcinoma Life Expectancy - Rare Disease Advisor\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"3f13bcf7a1b3e5ab\">>>", "url": "https://www.rarediseaseadvisor.com/hcp-resource/hepatocellular-carcinoma-life-expectancy/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a1d9fa9b38612acf\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIn recent years, the 5-year overall survival rate has improved from 18% to 22%,1 and <strong>may exceed 50%</strong> with currently available treatment options, according to a nationwide analysis conducted in France.2 · HCC accounts for approximately 90% of ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"a1d9fa9b38612acf\">>>", "published": "September 19, 2025", "siteName": "www.rarediseaseadvisor.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8f20a3ea85b67e35\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLiver Cancer Survival Rates | Roswell Park Comprehensive Cancer Center - Buffalo, NY\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8f20a3ea85b67e35\">>>", "url": "https://www.roswellpark.org/cancer/liver/survival-rates", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f7439eb7dceec64b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDistant disease. Among patients with disease that has spread to distant body areas at the time of diagnosis, 5-year survival is <strong>3.5%</strong>. Learn more liver cancer survival statistics from the National Cancer Institute.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f7439eb7dceec64b\">>>", "siteName": "www.roswellpark.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"71d6c69e9d270ac9\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLiver Cancer: Symptoms, Signs, Causes & Treatment\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"71d6c69e9d270ac9\">>>", "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9418-liver-cancer", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d48386d0ca68596a\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFurther, researchers break down survival rates by cancer stage. The five-year survival rate for early-stage (stage I or stage II) liver cancer is <strong>37%</strong>. That survival rate drops to 13% for stage III liver cancer and 3% for stage IV liver cancer.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d48386d0ca68596a\">>>", "published": "August 30, 2023", "siteName": "my.clevelandclinic.org"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1776349058181}}
{"type": "message", "id": "31dbbe26", "parentId": "20d40aba", "timestamp": "2026-04-16T14:17:38.183Z", "message": {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "functions.web_search:12", "toolName": "web_search", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\n  \"query\": \"stomach gastric cancer 5 year survival rate\",\n  \"provider\": \"brave\",\n  \"count\": 10,\n  \"tookMs\": 957,\n  \"externalContent\": {\n    \"untrusted\": true,\n    \"source\": \"web_search\",\n    \"provider\": \"brave\",\n    \"wrapped\": true\n  },\n  \"results\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b105c4460d6d6e9b\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStomach (Gastric) Cancer Survival Rates | American Cancer Society\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b105c4460d6d6e9b\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/stomach-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"47231c397f343dfb\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of stomach cancer is 70%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 70%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"47231c397f343dfb\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 27, 2026\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5d85a7c95d1f59ba\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStomach Cancer Survival Rates and Statistics - NCI\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5d85a7c95d1f59ba\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cancer.gov/types/stomach/survival\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"168b5e51cb67da3e\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor example, the 5-year relative survival rate for stomach cancer is <strong>36%</strong>. This means that, overall, people diagnosed with stomach cancer are <strong>36%</strong> as likely as similar people who do not have stomach cancer to be alive 5 years after diagnosis.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"168b5e51cb67da3e\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"008dbda35fd158c0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nRecent Estimates and Predictions of 5-Year Survival in Patients with Gastric Cancer: A Model-Based Period Analysis - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"008dbda35fd158c0\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9067041/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f65eb5951d10b753\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nDuring 2007–2016, the 5-year relative survival rate of patients with gastric cancer continued to rise, and the same trend was observed in 2017–2021. The 5-year overall age-standardized relative survival rates in 2007–2011, 2012–2016, and 2017–2021 were <strong>38.3%, 40.6%, and 42.9%</strong>, ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"f65eb5951d10b753\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d7d56d9c9f4d22e4\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nCancer of the Stomach - Cancer Stat Facts\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"d7d56d9c9f4d22e4\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/stomach.html\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fee8494bd0f33cac\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe earlier stomach cancer is caught, the better chance a person has of surviving five years after being diagnosed. For stomach cancer, <strong>30.7% are diagnosed at the local stage. The 5-year relative survival for localized stomach cancer is 76.5%</strong>.\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"fee8494bd0f33cac\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"seer.cancer.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"96ea61f8bd747a59\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLong-term relative survival of patients with gastric cancer from a large-scale cohort: a period-analysis - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"96ea61f8bd747a59\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11571998/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cc92c85c16cd6e00\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe survival rates for patients at all clinical stages have increased, with the 5-year relative survival rate for TNM stages I-III rising to <strong>89.7% in 2018–2022.</strong> Generally speaking, there has been a significant improvement in the 5-year relative survival rate for gastric cancer patients at ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"cc92c85c16cd6e00\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7dc108b8d8706541\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStomach cancer survival rate: Based on stage, sex, age, race\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"7dc108b8d8706541\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/stomach-cancer-survival-rate\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5c6d2632351c67bd\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nAccording to SEER, the 5-year relative survival rate for people with stomach cancer is · <strong>37.9%</strong>. This figure is based on data collected from 2015 to 2021. Data from the World Health Organization (WHO) indicates that stomach cancer, or gastric ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"5c6d2632351c67bd\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"July 8, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.medicalnewstoday.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e72c88accd78e21f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival in Stomach Cancer Is Improving: Results of a Nationwide Population-Based Swedish Study - PMC\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"e72c88accd78e21f\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1420858/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b58b162f83a748db\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe 5-year relative survival rate ... cancer of the gastric cardia, the 5-year relative survival rate increased from 4.7% (95% CI 2.3–7.1) to <strong>10.4% (95% CI 7.7–13.1),</strong> but the 10-year relative survival rate did not improve....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b58b162f83a748db\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b08ae220d9745c8f\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nStomach Cancer Survival | Roswell Park Comprehensive Cancer Center - Buffalo, NY\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"b08ae220d9745c8f\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.roswellpark.org/cancer/stomach/survival-rate\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6748b479fdd5c185\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nIn the United States, overall survival ... using three very broad categories: Localized disease. <strong>In patients with early-stage, localized cancers where the cancer is confined to the primary site, 5-year survival is 76.5%</strong>....\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"6748b479fdd5c185\\\">>>\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.roswellpark.org\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"23000c3148c5fbd5\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nLife expectancy of patients with early gastric cancer who underwent curative gastrectomy: comparison with the general population | Scientific Reports\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"23000c3148c5fbd5\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-89158-y\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"371d4ef2d6f30352\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nThe survival rate of patients with gastric cancer has also undergone gradual improvement, with the 5-year survival rate approximating <strong>73.2%</strong>. The survival rate for stage 1 gastric cancer is notably better, with reported 5-year survival rates ...\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"371d4ef2d6f30352\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"February 12, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"www.nature.com\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b6f24ea388baa51\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nSurvival Rate and Prognosis for Gastric Cancer\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8b6f24ea388baa51\\\">>>\",\n      \"url\": \"https://massivebio.com/survival-rate-and-prognosis-for-gastric-cancer-bio/\",\n      \"description\": \"\\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8887363550915fa0\\\">>>\\nSource: Web Search\\n---\\nFor instance, according to data from the National Cancer Institute’s SEER Program, the overall 5-year relative survival rate for stomach cancer in the United States is approximately <strong>33.3% for all stages combined</strong> (2013-2019 data).\\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\\\"8887363550915fa0\\\">>>\",\n      \"published\": \"December 5, 2025\",\n      \"siteName\": \"massivebio.com\"\n    }\n  ]\n}"}], "details": {"query": "stomach gastric cancer 5 year survival rate", "provider": "brave", "count": 10, "tookMs": 957, "externalContent": {"untrusted": true, "source": "web_search", "provider": "brave", "wrapped": true}, "results": [{"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b105c4460d6d6e9b\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStomach (Gastric) Cancer Survival Rates | American Cancer Society\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b105c4460d6d6e9b\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.org/cancer/types/stomach-cancer/detection-diagnosis-staging/survival-rates.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"47231c397f343dfb\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, if the 5-year relative survival rate for a specific stage of stomach cancer is 70%, it means that people who have that cancer are, on average, <strong>about 70%</strong> as likely as people who don’t have that cancer to live for at least 5 years after being diagnosed.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"47231c397f343dfb\">>>", "published": "February 27, 2026", "siteName": "www.cancer.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5d85a7c95d1f59ba\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStomach Cancer Survival Rates and Statistics - NCI\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5d85a7c95d1f59ba\">>>", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/types/stomach/survival", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"168b5e51cb67da3e\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor example, the 5-year relative survival rate for stomach cancer is <strong>36%</strong>. This means that, overall, people diagnosed with stomach cancer are <strong>36%</strong> as likely as similar people who do not have stomach cancer to be alive 5 years after diagnosis.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"168b5e51cb67da3e\">>>", "siteName": "www.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"008dbda35fd158c0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nRecent Estimates and Predictions of 5-Year Survival in Patients with Gastric Cancer: A Model-Based Period Analysis - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"008dbda35fd158c0\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9067041/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f65eb5951d10b753\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDuring 2007–2016, the 5-year relative survival rate of patients with gastric cancer continued to rise, and the same trend was observed in 2017–2021. The 5-year overall age-standardized relative survival rates in 2007–2011, 2012–2016, and 2017–2021 were <strong>38.3%, 40.6%, and 42.9%</strong>, ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"f65eb5951d10b753\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d7d56d9c9f4d22e4\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nCancer of the Stomach - Cancer Stat Facts\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"d7d56d9c9f4d22e4\">>>", "url": "https://seer.cancer.gov/statfacts/html/stomach.html", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fee8494bd0f33cac\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe earlier stomach cancer is caught, the better chance a person has of surviving five years after being diagnosed. For stomach cancer, <strong>30.7% are diagnosed at the local stage. The 5-year relative survival for localized stomach cancer is 76.5%</strong>.\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"fee8494bd0f33cac\">>>", "siteName": "seer.cancer.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"96ea61f8bd747a59\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLong-term relative survival of patients with gastric cancer from a large-scale cohort: a period-analysis - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"96ea61f8bd747a59\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11571998/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cc92c85c16cd6e00\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe survival rates for patients at all clinical stages have increased, with the 5-year relative survival rate for TNM stages I-III rising to <strong>89.7% in 2018–2022.</strong> Generally speaking, there has been a significant improvement in the 5-year relative survival rate for gastric cancer patients at ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"cc92c85c16cd6e00\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7dc108b8d8706541\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStomach cancer survival rate: Based on stage, sex, age, race\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"7dc108b8d8706541\">>>", "url": "https://www.medicalnewstoday.com/articles/stomach-cancer-survival-rate", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5c6d2632351c67bd\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nAccording to SEER, the 5-year relative survival rate for people with stomach cancer is · <strong>37.9%</strong>. This figure is based on data collected from 2015 to 2021. Data from the World Health Organization (WHO) indicates that stomach cancer, or gastric ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"5c6d2632351c67bd\">>>", "published": "July 8, 2025", "siteName": "www.medicalnewstoday.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e72c88accd78e21f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival in Stomach Cancer Is Improving: Results of a Nationwide Population-Based Swedish Study - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"e72c88accd78e21f\">>>", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1420858/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b58b162f83a748db\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe 5-year relative survival rate ... cancer of the gastric cardia, the 5-year relative survival rate increased from 4.7% (95% CI 2.3–7.1) to <strong>10.4% (95% CI 7.7–13.1),</strong> but the 10-year relative survival rate did not improve....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b58b162f83a748db\">>>", "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b08ae220d9745c8f\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nStomach Cancer Survival | Roswell Park Comprehensive Cancer Center - Buffalo, NY\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"b08ae220d9745c8f\">>>", "url": "https://www.roswellpark.org/cancer/stomach/survival-rate", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6748b479fdd5c185\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nIn the United States, overall survival ... using three very broad categories: Localized disease. <strong>In patients with early-stage, localized cancers where the cancer is confined to the primary site, 5-year survival is 76.5%</strong>....\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"6748b479fdd5c185\">>>", "siteName": "www.roswellpark.org"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"23000c3148c5fbd5\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nLife expectancy of patients with early gastric cancer who underwent curative gastrectomy: comparison with the general population | Scientific Reports\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"23000c3148c5fbd5\">>>", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-89158-y", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"371d4ef2d6f30352\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nThe survival rate of patients with gastric cancer has also undergone gradual improvement, with the 5-year survival rate approximating <strong>73.2%</strong>. The survival rate for stage 1 gastric cancer is notably better, with reported 5-year survival rates ...\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"371d4ef2d6f30352\">>>", "published": "February 12, 2025", "siteName": "www.nature.com"}, {"title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b6f24ea388baa51\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nSurvival Rate and Prognosis for Gastric Cancer\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8b6f24ea388baa51\">>>", "url": "https://massivebio.com/survival-rate-and-prognosis-for-gastric-cancer-bio/", "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8887363550915fa0\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nFor instance, according to data from the National Cancer Institute’s SEER Program, the overall 5-year relative survival rate for stomach cancer in the United States is approximately <strong>33.3% for all stages combined</strong> (2013-2019 data).\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"8887363550915fa0\">>>", "published": "December 5, 2025", "siteName": "massivebio.com"}]}, "isError": false, "timestamp": 1776349058182}}
